The provided solution is a refined Hybrid Automaton (HA) model for a buck       
converter, identified through trajectory analysis. The system consists of three 
modes: an initial transient mode (Mode 1), a switch-on mode (Mode 2), and a     
switch-off mode (Mode 3). The continuous dynamics in each mode are modeled as   
first-order linear ODEs. The high-frequency switching behavior, characteristic  
of the buck converter, is captured by guard conditions on the inductor current  
($x_1$).                                                                        
                                                                                
In Mode 2 (ON), the inductor current increases driven by a constant voltage term
(+8000), while in Mode 3 (OFF), it decreases. The output voltage ($x_2$) is the 
integral of the current minus a damping term representing the resistive load.   
The switching occurs when the current hits the upper bound (7.75) or lower bound
(-4.18), ensuring that the system remains in a limit-cycle oscillation.         
                                                                                
